4 × 100 metres Freestyle Relay, Women" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "4 –  6 August 1948"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/342"} "Wembley Arena, Wembley, London"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/5236"} "46 from 11 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "After the " [:a {:href "/results/5197"} "individual 100 metres"] " had been decided, it seemed logical that the medals would be decided between four teams: Denmark, the USA, the Netherlands, and Sweden."] "\n\n" [:p {} "With a rejuvenated " [:a {:href "/athletes/46251"} "Greta Andersen"] " included in their line-up, the Danes found themselves battling the Dutch for the gold medal as the last leg swimmers got under way. The United States and Great Britain trailed and appeared to be fighting only for the bronze, but the last American swimmer was " [:a {:href "/athletes/51000"} "Ann Curtis"] ". Curtis soon moved away from her British rival and began to make headway on the leaders. With just 15 metres to go, she overtook the Dutch swimmer and, with the crowd cheering wildly, edged even closer to " [:a {:href "/athletes/46259"} "Fritze Carstensen"] "  of Denmark. With a desperate final sprint, Curtis touched the wall ahead of the Dane to break the Olympic record and clinch the championship. Curtis’ time of 1:04.4 was the fastest ever yet recorded, though it could not be ratified as a world record because only the first leg of a relay is allowed to stand for record purposes."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} "United States"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: midd